1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,304
This practical lesson supports the school Week Five resuscitation
outline. It integrates all eight saved sections of the AET manual-
defibrillation supplement without repeating its patient story or
completing a school assessment.

2
00:00:14,304 --> 00:00:29,112
The supplement is matched by topic, not explicitly allocated in the
saved school directory. Its identical second entry is not another
course. The explicitly assigned Seven C Part A remains the weekly
module spine.

3
00:00:29,112 --> 00:00:42,720
Learn the decision and team sequence before memorizing a button. A
school laboratory may teach a manual workflow, while a service-
configured monitor can restrict which functions a particular operator
may use.

4
00:00:42,720 --> 00:01:00,552
The linked bilingual reading identifies exact BCPEG pages and current-
source differences. Device checkoffs, the actual school configuration
and the restricted current PCP operating procedure remain unverified,
so this is preparation, not credentialing.

5
00:01:00,552 --> 00:01:13,440
Defibrillation delivers an electrical intervention to terminate
certain dangerous ventricular rhythms. It does not pump blood for the
patient, correct every cause or guarantee that a perfusing rhythm will
follow.

6
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:27,528
The school three-phase model calls the early period electrical. It
helps explain the urgency of treating VF or pulseless VT while
myocardium is more viable, not a rule that every arrest starts in VF.

7
00:01:27,528 --> 00:01:39,192
If the defibrillator is available and a shock is indicated, do not
delay that shock to finish a fixed period of CPR simply because the
history suggests the patient is now in a later phase.

8
00:01:39,192 --> 00:01:53,496
Continue good CPR during preparation and minimize the necessary
assessment and shock pause. Early CPR and early defibrillation support
each other; they are not competing treatments from which to choose
only one.

9
00:01:53,496 --> 00:02:08,856
Arrest deprives cells of oxygen and metabolic substrate, reducing
their ability to sustain electrical and mechanical function.
Compressions generate limited circulation while definitive treatment
and cause correction are organized.

10
00:02:08,856 --> 00:02:21,648
Interruptions reduce perfusion pressure. Restarting compressions does
not instantly restore everything lost during a pause. This explains
the importance of planning equipment and exchanges before stopping.

11
00:02:21,648 --> 00:02:35,232
The school gives illustrative minute and pressure-recovery figures.
They are not exact physiological clocks for every patient, and they do
not establish an obligatory fifteen-second wait before assessing an
indicated rhythm.

12
00:02:35,232 --> 00:02:47,112
Measure actual hands-off intervals in practice. Improve an eight-
second pause to a shorter safe pause rather than accepting eight
seconds merely because it sits below a published upper bound.

13
00:02:47,112 --> 00:03:01,224
The later metabolic phase describes worsening cellular injury and
complex injury after circulation returns. It is useful physiology, but
a ten-minute boundary does not prove irreversible death in an
individual.

14
00:03:01,224 --> 00:03:14,040
Arrest duration is only one part of the history. Distinguish time
without any circulation-supporting CPR from time with effective CPR,
and recognize special causes such as hypothermia.

15
00:03:14,040 --> 00:03:25,896
Do not teach the school survival-per-minute percentage as a
personalized prognosis, or a progression from coarse VF to fine VF to
asystole as a mandatory sequence in every case.

16
00:03:25,896 --> 00:03:40,320
Withholding, discontinuation and recognition of life extinct follow
their actual pathways. The preceding Seven C lecture teaches those
distinctions; a phase label or monitor appearance does not replace
them.

17
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:54,792
The chain of survival includes recognition, response activation, CPR,
defibrillation when indicated, advanced care and post-arrest recovery.
Completing a later link does not make an earlier link unnecessary.

18
00:03:54,792 --> 00:04:08,040
For example, an access operator can prepare a line while the
compressor works and the defibrillator operator prepares pads. Access
must not become the reason the first useful shock is delayed.

19
00:04:08,040 --> 00:04:22,104
Use additional personnel where they improve a specific task. If there
are fewer people, combine roles deliberately and prioritize lifesaving
actions rather than trying to perform every textbook task
simultaneously.

20
00:04:22,104 --> 00:04:33,408
A useful leader continually asks what is preventing effective oxygen
delivery now. That question connects technique to the patient instead
of turning the arrest into a checklist race.

21
00:04:33,408 --> 00:04:48,984
Allocate compression, airway, defibrillator, leadership and recording
functions. Add medication or access roles according to people and
authority. A role title alone does not confirm that its essential
tasks are being done.

22
00:04:48,984 --> 00:05:01,968
Say a name and a concrete action, receive a read-back, then expect
completion or a barrier report. If a device is not ready, that fact
must reach the leader before a planned pause begins.

23
00:05:01,968 --> 00:05:13,512
The compressor should announce fatigue and the next operator should be
ready before the exchange. The recorder can warn of the approaching
cycle boundary without dictating an unsafe interruption.

24
00:05:13,512 --> 00:05:25,632
When new responders arrive, give a short patient-state and treatment
summary before assigning a gap. An unbriefed extra person at the chest
can create confusion rather than useful capacity.

25
00:05:25,632 --> 00:05:40,104
For this adult practical, combine a rate of one hundred to one hundred
twenty with appropriate five to six centimeter depth, full recoil and
minimal interruptions. A metronome checks timing, not all of these
dimensions.

26
00:05:40,104 --> 00:05:54,240
Keep shoulders and hands appropriately aligned and avoid leaning
between compressions. Full recoil means releasing downward pressure,
not repeatedly removing all contact and losing the correct location.

27
00:05:54,240 --> 00:06:06,912
Use a suitable firm support and the actual training feedback. Rotate
about every two minutes, or sooner if quality declines, coordinating
the transition with a necessary pause whenever feasible.

28
00:06:06,912 --> 00:06:17,592
An impressive compression fraction can still coexist with shallow or
poorly positioned compressions. Review the whole performance rather
than celebrating one favorable number.

29
00:06:17,592 --> 00:06:33,144
In the current local adult pathway, use thirty compressions and two
breaths without an advanced airway. With an appropriate advanced
airway, continuous compressions and roughly one breath each six
seconds are the relevant adult framework.

30
00:06:33,144 --> 00:06:46,200
Coordinate ventilation with the compressor and avoid unnecessarily
long pauses. Deliver enough to produce appropriate chest rise, not an
aggressive squeeze intended to inflate the lungs as much as possible.

31
00:06:46,200 --> 00:07:00,912
Excessive rate or volume raises intrathoracic pressure and can impair
venous return. Hypocapnia after ROSC can also reduce cerebral blood
flow; faster breathing is not automatically better oxygen delivery.

32
00:07:00,912 --> 00:07:17,184
If the waveform changes or chest rise is poor, evaluate airway
position, obstruction, seal and the device while the team maintains
the necessary arrest care. Do not solve an unexplained low CO2 by
blindly increasing ventilation.

33
00:07:17,184 --> 00:07:30,456
An AED analyzes a signal through its specified electrodes and advises
or delivers treatment according to its design. A semiautomatic AED
still requires an operator to press the shock control when prompted.

34
00:07:30,456 --> 00:07:44,880
Manual operation moves rhythm interpretation and energy selection to a
trained, authorized person. Skilled use may shorten pauses, but a
confident misinterpretation can also produce an inappropriate shock.

35
00:07:44,880 --> 00:07:57,504
Biphasic describes reversal of current direction during the waveform.
It does not make every manufacturer use the same joules or mean that a
device automatically delivers only the energy the patient needs.

36
00:07:57,504 --> 00:08:09,552
Do not delay an available, appropriate AED shock just to replace it
with a preferred manual device. Coordinate any transition so that
working CPR and defibrillation are not lost.

37
00:08:09,552 --> 00:08:21,576
Confirm whether the device is in manual therapy, AED prompted protocol
or advisory monitoring. Similar-looking screens can support different
functions and different operator permissions.

38
00:08:21,576 --> 00:08:35,280
The official LIFEPAK manual describes settings that can restrict
manual access, require confirmation or require a passcode. This is a
device capability description, not a reason to circumvent
restrictions.

39
00:08:35,280 --> 00:08:47,016
The current linked BCEHS PCP operating procedure is login restricted
and has not been read. We do not infer its present settings from an
old factory default or from a school supplement.

40
00:08:47,016 --> 00:09:00,288
Synchronized cardioversion and pacing are advanced interventions in
this context. Knowing what SYNC or PACER means helps avoid errors; it
does not authorize a learner to initiate those therapies.

41
00:09:00,288 --> 00:09:12,216
Check device readiness, power and compatible in-date pads and cable.
The ECG cable used to monitor a rhythm is not automatically the
therapy connection used to deliver a shock.

42
00:09:12,216 --> 00:09:26,712
Expose and prepare enough chest for correct pad placement while
preserving dignity where possible. Remove obstructing clothing and
moisture and address excessive hair without turning preparation into a
prolonged pause.

43
00:09:26,712 --> 00:09:40,032
Use the device instructions for skin preparation, approved accessories
and connections. Do not put extra paddle gel under adhesive pads or
improvise an incompatible connector to save a few seconds.

44
00:09:40,032 --> 00:09:52,320
Announce readiness before the team commits to an assessment pause. If
the cable remains disconnected, continue useful CPR while that problem
is fixed instead of making everyone wait with hands off.

45
00:09:52,320 --> 00:10:05,208
For the adult anterior-lateral arrangement, place one electrode on the
upper right chest below the clavicle and the other on the left lateral
chest with the pad center near the midaxillary line as appropriate.

46
00:10:05,208 --> 00:10:20,328
Adapt to actual anatomy, avoid obstructing patches or implanted-device
sites when possible, and obtain full skin contact. A pad balanced on a
fold or only partly attached may not transmit energy effectively.

47
00:10:20,328 --> 00:10:35,304
The reviewed LP15 instructions distinguish an anterior-posterior
option for manual therapy from its anterior-lateral requirement in AED
mode. Do not turn an alternative placement from one mode into a
universal rule for another.

48
00:10:35,304 --> 00:10:49,104
Inspect adhesion before discharge and after movement. If an electrode
must be moved, follow its replacement instructions rather than
assuming a lifted, dried pad can always be stuck back with equal
performance.

49
00:10:49,104 --> 00:11:01,776
The school supplement uses two hundred joules as its initial adult
LIFEPAK example. Learn that labelled school example, but do not export
it as the one initial dose for every biphasic defibrillator.

50
00:11:01,776 --> 00:11:18,096
For real use, the authorized operator follows the applicable protocol
and exact model configuration, including any escalation. A numbered
setting, a charged energy display and an actual delivered-shock record
are different observations.

51
00:11:18,096 --> 00:11:32,616
Confirm the intended setting before charging and confirm available
energy before discharge. A mechanical patient twitch is not reliable
proof of delivered energy, and absence of ROSC is not proof of
equipment failure.

52
00:11:32,616 --> 00:11:46,776
Do not use a universal energy range from a general nursing table, or
assume smaller pads automatically reduce manual energy. Those
shortcuts can turn an otherwise coordinated workflow into an incorrect
treatment.

53
00:11:46,776 --> 00:12:00,792
The school COACHED sequence keeps compressions going while the
operator prepares. It calls out oxygen management, others clear,
charging, hands off, rhythm evaluation and either defibrillation or
disarming.

54
00:12:00,792 --> 00:12:14,640
Translate each word into an observed action. Oxygen away requires the
airway person to manage the actual oxygen source, not somebody merely
repeating the phrase while free-flow oxygen remains at the chest.

55
00:12:14,640 --> 00:12:31,440
The supplement gives a one-meter free-flow oxygen example; the
reviewed manufacturer warning requires managing nearby oxygen sources.
Follow the actual equipment fire-safety procedure rather than
indiscriminately disconnecting every secured ventilation circuit.

56
00:12:31,440 --> 00:12:43,704
The person still compressing must explicitly acknowledge clearing
before discharge. This is why a memorized rapid chant cannot replace a
visual check of people, patient and equipment.

57
00:12:43,704 --> 00:12:56,328
In the taught manual workflow, charging can occur while compressions
continue. Nobody should interpret a charge tone as an instruction to
deliver an immediate shock with the compressor still touching the
patient.

58
00:12:56,328 --> 00:13:11,712
Before discharge, ensure no person is touching the patient, stretcher,
bed or conductive equipment in contact with the patient. Look for an
unnoticed hand, a helper holding a limb or a rescuer leaning on a
rail.

59
00:13:11,712 --> 00:13:23,856
Adhesive pads are not hand-held paddles. Do not press on adhesive
therapy pads during discharge or copy a paddle-pressure instruction
onto a hands-free electrode workflow.

60
00:13:23,856 --> 00:13:36,000
A last-second safety concern overrides the desire for a fast pause.
Stop the unsafe action, make the situation safe and minimize the
additional interruption without skipping the check.

61
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:49,296
A rhythm assessment requires an interpretable ECG. Compression and
movement artifact can resemble ventricular activity; a dramatic screen
during active compressions is not enough to select a shock.

62
00:13:49,296 --> 00:14:05,520
At the coordinated brief assessment, identify VF, pulseless VT,
asystole or an organized pattern in the patient context. Pulselessness
is a clinical state, not an automatic label attached to every wide
complex.

63
00:14:05,520 --> 00:14:17,640
State the decision clearly so the compressor and recorder know what
follows. An organized rhythm alone does not justify repeated prolonged
pulse checks outside the appropriate assessment sequence.

64
00:14:17,640 --> 00:14:31,536
Aim for the shortest safe assessment and shock pause. If equipment
uncertainty cannot be resolved immediately, maintain necessary CPR and
address the specific uncertainty rather than prolonging hands-off
debate.

65
00:14:31,536 --> 00:14:43,368
When the rhythm is shockable and safety and energy are verified, the
operator delivers the shock using the correct control. Announce
delivery so everyone understands the transition.

66
00:14:43,368 --> 00:14:55,008
Immediately restart compressions after the shock in the ongoing arrest
pathway. Do not spend the next interval staring at the screen or
routinely checking for a pulse just to see whether the shock worked.

67
00:14:55,008 --> 00:15:05,688
Continue ventilation, planned exchanges and investigation of causes
without allowing the delivered shock to become an unstructured pause
in all other treatment.

68
00:15:05,688 --> 00:15:20,616
Record the actual delivered energy and time, not just what was
selected. If spontaneous signs suggest a real state change,
communicate and assess appropriately rather than ignoring the patient
to finish a fixed cycle blindly.

69
00:15:20,616 --> 00:15:32,832
A precharged device does not commit the team to a shock. If assessment
does not support defibrillation, announce that decision and remove the
unwanted charge through the device disarm procedure.

70
00:15:32,832 --> 00:15:46,608
The reviewed LIFEPAK manual describes pressing the speed dial to
cancel a charge. It also describes automatic disarming under certain
conditions, but waiting for a timeout is not the same as deliberately
securing the device.

71
00:15:46,608 --> 00:15:58,752
Do not discharge into the air or give an unnecessary patient shock
merely to empty the capacitor. Verify the actual disarmed state rather
than assuming the phrase charge dumped completed the action.

72
00:15:58,752 --> 00:16:09,648
Resume necessary CPR promptly after the assessment. The operator can
confirm device status while the team returns to nonshockable care and
cause-directed thinking.

73
00:16:09,648 --> 00:16:23,448
True asystole and PEA are nonshockable arrest states. Continue
effective CPR, ventilation and appropriate cause management;
increasing joules does not turn them into indications for
defibrillation.

74
00:16:23,448 --> 00:16:35,472
An AED no-shock message does not establish a pulse, prove good signal
quality or certify death. Combine the message with the patient
assessment and troubleshoot contradictory findings.

75
00:16:35,472 --> 00:16:49,248
PEA can appear narrow or wide, fast or slow. The important immediate
distinction is organized electrical activity without effective
circulation, not memorizing one single strip appearance.

76
00:16:49,248 --> 00:17:00,408
Continue the applicable reassessment cycle and early consultation
planning. A single nonshockable assessment is not a new timer-based
permission to terminate resuscitation.

77
00:17:00,408 --> 00:17:14,760
For an unexpected trace, check the patient and the signal chain: skin
contact, compatible cable, electrode connection, displayed source and
motion. Do not assume every irregular waveform is VF.

78
00:17:14,760 --> 00:17:29,424
The LP15 AED warning requires stopping a moving vehicle for analysis
and standing clear. Manual interpretation is also affected by
artifact; changing the mode does not magically create a reliable ECG.

79
00:17:29,424 --> 00:17:43,560
If pads do not adhere, correct the cause and replace unsuitable pads
according to the instructions. If the monitor loses power, assign
troubleshooting or a backup while the rest of the team sustains
essential care.

80
00:17:43,560 --> 00:17:56,568
Avoid a long global stop while several people inspect the same
connector. Say the precise fault and assign one owner, with a clear
plan for rhythm assessment and defibrillation as soon as feasible.

81
00:17:56,568 --> 00:18:09,360
A flat-looking screen may represent asystole, a disconnected source,
an unsuitable display setting or low-amplitude activity. Do not
diagnose death from the first glance at one trace.

82
00:18:09,360 --> 00:18:23,520
Inspect connection and source, and use appropriate leads or display
assessment according to the device and local procedure. The goal is a
reliable interpretation, not adjusting the screen until it resembles a
shockable rhythm.

83
00:18:23,520 --> 00:18:35,808
Do not reflexively shock an uncertain flat line. Keep the assessment
focused and brief, return to useful CPR and obtain the needed
expertise without unnecessary delay.

84
00:18:35,808 --> 00:18:47,280
Document what was actually visible and what checks were made. Do not
write confirmed asystole simply because the initial electrode was
disconnected or the display was unreadable.

85
00:18:47,280 --> 00:18:59,376
Defibrillation, synchronized cardioversion and transcutaneous pacing
are not interchangeable settings for a sick patient. They address
different electrical and clinical problems.

86
00:18:59,376 --> 00:19:12,456
A wide fast rhythm with a definite pulse is not automatically
pulseless VT. Assess hemodynamic instability and request the
appropriate advanced help rather than choosing the arrest shock
pathway from width alone.

87
00:19:12,456 --> 00:19:24,384
A very slow perfusing rhythm may need urgent advanced treatment. It is
not corrected by defibrillating a nonshockable rhythm or improvising
an unsupervised pacing setup.

88
00:19:24,384 --> 00:19:38,400
In this PCP learner context, recognition, supportive care, early
escalation and preparation are the task. Device capability and an
educational explanation do not expand procedural authority.

89
00:19:38,400 --> 00:19:52,488
The school supplement repeatedly generalizes that AEDs are not
intended for children under eight. The reviewed LP15 AED mode does
have that restriction; AEDs in general do not share a universal under-
eight ban.

90
00:19:52,488 --> 00:20:06,264
Current AHA and AAP guidance supports pediatric attenuation when
available, trained manual defibrillation where appropriate, and
consideration of a nonattenuated AED if the preferred options are
unavailable.

91
00:20:06,264 --> 00:20:19,920
Pads must fit without overlap and be compatible with the actual
equipment. On the reviewed LP15, attaching pediatric electrodes does
not automatically select a weight-based energy dose.

92
00:20:19,920 --> 00:20:33,960
This comparison prevents an unsafe generalization; it is not the
complete pediatric course. Keep age-specific assessment, ventilation,
energy and device training together rather than exporting adult
defaults.

93
00:20:33,960 --> 00:20:47,472
The supplement describes broad IO use in arrest and shock. Current
local PR12 is more specific for independent PCP IO and distinguishes
direct on-scene advanced supervision from a telephone consultation.

94
00:20:47,472 --> 00:21:01,440
The previously taught independent branch is not every medical arrest:
it includes the specified hypovolemic-arrest context, critical fluid
need and unavailable or failed IV, with the other current limits.

95
00:21:01,440 --> 00:21:15,576
PCP tibial site limits, age restrictions and collective attempt limits
are not erased by a school humeral or pediatric example. Access does
not automatically grant epinephrine or another advanced drug
authority.

96
00:21:15,576 --> 00:21:27,624
Use the Week Three vascular practical for the checked technical
sequence. Here the operational question is who can perform which
access task without delaying compressions or the indicated shock.

97
00:21:27,624 --> 00:21:43,128
The supplement access sequence contains conflicting statements about
flushing before versus after pain management. In an awake patient, use
the actual analgesia and confirmation procedure rather than copying
the first numbered list.

98
00:21:43,128 --> 00:21:57,552
Swelling, leakage or concerning resistance requires immediate
attention. Do not pressurize a questionable line harder to achieve a
nominal flow, and do not treat absent aspiration alone as automatic
proof of failure.

99
00:21:57,552 --> 00:22:11,256
Secure and repeatedly inspect any functioning access, especially after
movement. Distinguish device assistance from certainty: a pressure
infuser cannot confirm that the tip is correctly positioned.

100
00:22:11,256 --> 00:22:25,128
Communicate failure or an uncertain delivered dose accurately.
Document the site, attempt, findings, action and response; do not
invent a successful infusion because a bag volume decreased.

101
00:22:25,128 --> 00:22:39,984
A sudden sustained ETCO2 rise can suggest ROSC, but no universal
thirty-five or forty millimeter threshold proves it. Ventilation,
drugs, compression quality and sampling can also change the number.

102
00:22:39,984 --> 00:22:55,320
Communicate the clue and assess circulation appropriately. Once ROSC
is confirmed, announce the transition so compressions, ventilation,
monitoring and recording are intentionally coordinated rather than
independently changed.

103
00:22:55,320 --> 00:23:12,240
Titrate oxygen with reliable measurement, avoid excessive ventilation,
assess blood pressure and perfusion, maintain airway support and
prepare for recurrence. The preceding lesson labels the actual N03 and
other-source target differences.

104
00:23:12,240 --> 00:23:27,888
Temperature management, cause-directed destination and early ECG
communication remain important. Do not import a hospital temperature
protocol, routine cold fluid or a prognostic judgment from one early
neurological examination.

105
00:23:27,888 --> 00:23:40,368
The LP15 advisory configuration allows monitoring functions while a
background system looks for potentially shockable rhythms. It is not
simply full manual therapy with a safety net switched on.

106
00:23:40,368 --> 00:23:55,272
The reviewed manual says surveillance does not prompt for nonshockable
rhythms such as asystole. A perfusing organized rhythm and PEA can
also look electrically similar, so absence of an alert cannot
establish safety.

107
00:23:55,272 --> 00:24:10,536
A check-patient alert requires checking the patient. Motion, low
signal amplitude and other problems can cause false alerts; do not
press ANALYZE or shock a conscious perfusing person just because the
device requested attention.

108
00:24:10,536 --> 00:24:26,184
In the reviewed configured workflow, LEAD returns from the AED
prompted protocol to advisory monitoring. Verify the actual displayed
mode and local configuration; this action does not prove entry into
unrestricted manual operation.

109
00:24:26,184 --> 00:24:42,408
An ECG tone can reflect detected QRS activity without useful
perfusion, and artifact or pacing signals can confuse rate displays.
Continue direct patient assessment instead of equating every beep with
a heartbeat that pumps blood.

110
00:24:42,408 --> 00:24:56,784
A pulse-oximeter tone depends on a usable optical signal. Poor
perfusion, motion and other limitations can make it unreliable during
arrest. SpO2 also does not measure carbon dioxide clearance.

111
00:24:56,784 --> 00:25:11,952
The reviewed LP15 offers SpO2 volume adjustment through its parameter
menu. Know the actual unit, keep alerts usable and avoid teaching an
unverified menu path as universal across models or software.

112
00:25:11,952 --> 00:25:28,272
A metronome provides timing, not depth, recoil or indication for CPR.
Its activity can also alter alarm behavior on the reviewed device.
Verify the monitoring state again when the patient transitions to
ROSC.

113
00:25:28,272 --> 00:25:41,304
A twelve-lead ECG uses ten electrodes to derive twelve views. The six
chest leads are not six augmented limb leads, and a three-lead rhythm
trace cannot replace a diagnostic twelve-lead.

114
00:25:41,304 --> 00:25:56,928
Enter correct patient information and the demographic fields required
by the actual system. The reviewed LP15 uses default assumptions if
age or sex is omitted; machine unconfirmed text is not an independent
diagnosis.

115
00:25:56,928 --> 00:26:13,416
After ROSC, obtain a useful ECG at the clinically appropriate time and
repeat if needed; the preceding lecture explains early-ECG timing
differences. An early post-arrest trace can be misleading, so maintain
the patient context.

116
00:26:13,416 --> 00:26:28,320
Send to the correct receiving pathway and confirm receipt or report
failure. A send icon, a printed strip and hospital acceptance are not
the same event, and a normal-looking ECG does not erase concerning
symptoms.

117
00:26:28,320 --> 00:26:43,416
The supplement describes importing monitor data into Siren and
manually completing medication details and ECG times. Treat those as
the described school workflow, not proof that every current software
version behaves identically.

118
00:26:43,416 --> 00:26:57,168
The reviewed EVENT menu records selected annotations; it does not
automatically know every compressor exchange, drug dose or clinical
decision. A generic event mark is not a complete medication entry.

119
00:26:57,168 --> 00:27:14,376
Reconcile onset and CPR history, rhythm analyses, shock time and
energy, ventilation/access, actual drug details, patient response,
consultation and handover. Distinguish directly observed facts from
reports by a bystander.

120
00:27:14,376 --> 00:27:28,848
If an imported time is wrong or an event is uncertain, use the
permitted correction process and explain the uncertainty. Do not
invent exactness, silently change history or claim that documentation
guarantees legal protection.

121
00:27:28,848 --> 00:27:45,336
The supplement mentions advisory monitoring during stable inter-
facility transfers. Current PR33 describes a prescreened stable
population with defined observations and consultation triggers; it is
not blanket clearance for every monitored patient.

122
00:27:45,336 --> 00:27:59,688
A newly resuscitated patient can remain critically unstable despite an
organized rhythm and one acceptable pressure. Continue close
assessment, resource planning and the applicable post-arrest
destination decision.

123
00:27:59,688 --> 00:28:15,264
During an authorized IFT, new chest pain, reduced consciousness,
hypotension or severe breathlessness changes the situation. Use the
actual consultation and destination response rather than waiting for a
shockable-rhythm alarm.

124
00:28:15,264 --> 00:28:28,224
Explain the distinction in a handover: baseline stability, what
changed, what was measured, what was done and what help is now
required. The mode name on the monitor does not define patient
stability.

125
00:28:28,224 --> 00:28:41,136
The saved supplement also connects monitor skills to ACS and stroke.
These are recognition and escalation applications, not evidence that
every time-critical call belongs in the arrest pathway.

126
00:28:41,136 --> 00:28:59,160
For suspected ACS, combine symptoms, perfusion and a quality ECG,
prepare for deterioration and use the appropriate notification
pathway. Neither absence of classic chest pain nor a nondiagnostic
first ECG safely excludes ACS.

127
00:28:59,160 --> 00:29:14,616
For possible stroke, a focused screen, exact last-known-well history
and glucose assessment support the next decision. A normal glucose
does not rule out stroke, and a normal FAST screen does not exclude
every stroke presentation.

128
00:29:14,616 --> 00:29:29,400
Do not let equipment setup consume the time needed for urgent support,
destination planning and pre-alert. The full ACS and stroke pathways
require their own source-based teaching, not an improvised answer from
an arrest slide.

129
00:29:29,400 --> 00:29:42,624
In this original drill, a fifty-four-year-old collapses at a community
workshop. Bystanders started CPR and an AED is operating. Do not
remove a working treatment before the replacement is ready.

130
00:29:42,624 --> 00:29:56,976
A cable fault delays the incoming manual unit. Assign one person to
solve that fault while the team maintains the current effective CPR
and follows the usable AED workflow. Do not create an avoidable no-
treatment interval.

131
00:29:56,976 --> 00:30:13,488
After a coordinated transition, apply the appropriate shock or no-
shock sequence from the actual assessment. Later, communicate the CO2
rise and movement, assess circulation appropriately and announce a
confirmed transition if supported.

132
00:30:13,488 --> 00:30:29,088
The recorder now reconciles bystander reports, device changes,
delivered therapy and the patient response. Success in this drill
means safe reasoning and coordinated action, not making the story end
with ROSC every time.

133
00:30:29,088 --> 00:30:47,688
After the event, compare the actual timeline with patient needs: first
effective CPR, first indicated shock, interruptions, airway
performance, causes, consultation and handover. A monitor download
informs this review but cannot reconstruct every human decision.

134
00:30:47,688 --> 00:31:01,680
Discuss system factors, skill gaps and communication using observed
facts. Avoid judging the entire team only by survival, and do not
promise that an operational learning review eliminates all
accountability.

135
00:31:01,680 --> 00:31:14,040
Choose a concrete improvement, such as preparing compatible connectors
before an exchange or shortening a specific pause without losing the
all-clear check. Rehearse the change and measure again.

136
00:31:14,040 --> 00:31:28,344
Separate operational learning from personal psychological support.
Offer appropriate voluntary resources without compelling a traumatic
retelling or promising absolute confidentiality beyond the applicable
framework.

137
00:31:28,344 --> 00:32:09,624
The literal respiratory pages connect appearance, history and
examination. Observe effort, speech, chest expansion and retractions;
compare both sides systematically for air entry and added sounds. Ask
about baseline disease, inhaled medicines, smoking or other exposures,
cough and the amount, colour and consistency of sputum. Compare with
what is usual for this patient. Asymmetry, crackles or wheeze narrows
questions but does not identify one disease automatically. Prioritize
an unstable patient rather than finishing the routine respiratory
count or interview.

138
00:32:09,624 --> 00:32:50,256
With an existing chest drain, include tube security, the actual
drainage system, breathing findings and possible subcutaneous air in
the focused assessment. This is not permission to clamp, disconnect or
manipulate an unfamiliar drain. The chapter respiratory-rate examples
depend on age, and its usual saturation above 95 percent is background
teaching rather than a universal oxygen prescription. Link an abnormal
observation to the applicable assessment and support pathway, then
report the response. Hospital nursing diagnoses and ordered blood-gas
sampling do not create a new paramedic procedure.

139
00:32:50,256 --> 00:33:27,672
The topical checklist begins with a verified order and medication
record, not a jar that happens to be nearby. Confirm identity with
appropriate independent identifiers; connect the intended medicine,
dose, route, time and indication to the actual patient, and document
what was really done. The source repeats label comparisons at
selection, preparation and before administration. Multiple signatures
on a record do not make an unclear order reliable. Stop and clarify a
mismatch rather than completing a ritual check and proceeding anyway.

140
00:33:27,672 --> 00:34:13,176
Topical treatment still requires protection and a product-specific
technique. Inspect the intended site, allergies and skin condition;
use appropriate gloves and hand hygiene to protect the patient and
avoid absorbing medication yourself. The checklist describes clearing
previous product, preparing the skin and applying the ordered quantity
without vigorous rubbing. Its suggestions about damp skin, a thin
versus generous layer, warming a product in gloved hands or sterile
gloves for lesions are not one instruction for every medicine, wound
or formulation. Follow the actual product and aseptic wound-care plan;
do not add heat or massage to a patch by analogy.

141
00:34:13,176 --> 00:34:53,352
Protect the remaining product as well as the application site. Use the
appropriate clean or sterile applicator without returning contaminated
material to the container. Change soiled gloves and clean hands when
moving to a clean task. Explain expected sensations, preserve comfort
and privacy, and record site, product, dose or amount, time and
patient response. A topical, transdermal, oral or injected formulation
is not interchangeable merely because the medicine name is similar.
Reconciliation includes what the patient already used, not just the
next intended dose.

142
00:34:53,352 --> 00:35:40,896
The literal range then opens parenteral administration. Intradermal
targets the dermis, subcutaneous the tissue beneath it, intramuscular
muscle and intravenous an existing venous route or access device. IV
delivery avoids an absorption step; an IM or SC injection still needs
absorption and is not guaranteed to act instantly. The source image
sketches IM at 90 degrees, SC at 45, IV at 25 and ID at 10-15. These
are schematic examples, not universal settings: patient tissue,
needle, device, medicine and the specifically trained technique
determine the actual approach. This opening range does not teach every
later injection checklist.

143
00:35:40,896 --> 00:36:21,312
Parenteral safety separates contamination, wrong-drug errors, sharps
injury and discomfort. Prepare in a suitable clean area, protect
sterile key parts, use equipment for its intended single-patient
purpose and dispose of sharps safely. Changing a needle does not make
a used syringe safe for another patient. The assigned table starts
hand hygiene and gives an old pump-count/time example; use the actual
hand-rub instructions, cover the hands and rub until dry instead of
assuming every dispenser delivers the same amount. Gloves never
replace hand hygiene before an aseptic task or after glove removal.

144
00:36:21,312 --> 00:37:05,280
Original rehearsal: a deteriorating patient has a documented
treatment, an open cream container with an unclear label and an IV
already running. First address airway, breathing and perfusion, then
establish what product and dose were actually used, inspect the line
and clarify the unclear preparation before further administration.
State which findings are observed, which are reported and which remain
unknown. The assignment includes chapter references and the next
chapter objectives; it does not require memorizing bibliography
entries or inventing missing orders. Explain how the assessment,
medicine checks and infusion chain protect this same patient.

145
00:37:05,280 --> 00:37:44,256
The recovered March 2025 page-40 box says thirty minutes have elapsed,
without the extra qualifications found in the June 2026 comparison.
That later page distinguishes thirty without ROSC or defibrillation
from forty if either occurred. Neither box is the complete current
BCEHS decision pathway. Current R02 uses its own eligibility,
consultation and rapid or minimum-duration branches. Keep the three
sources distinct; a timer alone does not authorize stopping, and this
comparison is not an instruction to wait before starting indicated
care.

146
00:37:44,256 --> 00:38:27,912
The 2025 pages 42-43 ask for oversight after twenty minutes of high-
quality CPR, when a treatable cause is identified, or when arrest
occurs during transport. Page 45 links a possible moving-CPR decision
to history, examination and consultation. Current R02 instead requires
an early CliniCall for an EMR or PCP leading without ACP support after
CPR, analysis and indicated defibrillation have begun; this may be
several cycles. A request for guidance is different from a decision to
terminate. Do not wait for a textbook twenty-minute point if the
actual current pathway requires earlier contact.

147
00:38:27,912 --> 00:39:13,920
The recovered 2025 nitrate card ties one dose to the strength of the
patient prescription, with a three-to-five-minute interval and
pressure strictly above 110. Its notes require oversight when there is
no prescription; the chest-pain page also requires consultation before
more than three doses. The June 2026 comparison specifies 0.4
milligrams sublingually. Current BCEHS likewise specifies 0.4
milligrams, with its own pressure-at-least-110 and heart-rate screen
and consultation requirements. Do not turn one source into an
unlabelled dose for another, or let a restarted pain episode erase
prior intake or contraindications.

148
00:39:13,920 --> 00:40:00,984
Original source-selection exercise: a patient remains in arrest at
thirty minutes, and a teammate points to the recovered single-line
box. Explain what is missing before any discontinuation decision: the
actual applicable pathway, rhythm and ROSC history, treatable causes,
clinical circumstances and consultation or standing direction. Then
repeat the exercise for nitrate treatment when the prescription
strength or previous doses are unclear. In both cases, clarify the
real source and patient information instead of choosing the most
permissive sentence. The 2025 page also lacks the later pediatric-pad
sentence; pediatric AED and LP15 restrictions remain a separately
labelled comparison.

149
00:40:00,984 --> 00:40:12,888
Close the lesson by talking through one full rhythm assessment from
ongoing CPR to the next intervention. Name who performs each action
and what evidence supports the decision.

150
00:40:12,888 --> 00:40:22,392
Then change the rhythm to nonshockable after charging. Explain
disarming and prompt CPR rather than delivering a shock just because
the device is ready.

151
00:40:22,392 --> 00:40:33,168
Next remove the advisory alarm while the patient becomes pulseless.
Explain why no alarm does not prove circulation and how direct
assessment changes the plan.

152
00:40:33,168 --> 00:40:46,632
Finally distinguish school examples, manufacturer instructions,
operational authority and current clinical pathways. That separation
is part of competence, not an optional footnote after memorizing the
technique.
